add([1580,1582],'class-count','Classifier outputs versus label schema','If the intended label schema has exactly the seven displayed categories, what must be checked about the eight-output classifier illustration?', ['The output head and label mapping must be made consistent with the selected seven-class task','The extra output automatically becomes a valid eighth emotion without a label','Softmax silently removes an unused output without any design change','The seven labels must be interpreted as eight because a dense layer exists'], 'An eight-output diagram cannot define a seven-class label mapping by itself; the task schema determines the output dimension.', ['An output index needs an explicitly defined target meaning.','Softmax normalizes all supplied logits and does not delete a class.','The number of explicitly defined labels remains seven.'],typ='scenario',kind='figure',conflict='Page1580 shows Dense Output8, while page1582 presents a seven-label example; these are not silently the same task.')
add(1581,'dataset-targets','Categorical and continuous dataset targets','Which target combination does the slide specifically identify for AffectNet?', ['Expression labels plus valence and arousal','Only raw audio waveforms and word timestamps','Only robot joint angles and torque labels','Only unlabeled face images with no affect annotations'], 'The dataset is described as supporting both discrete expression categories and continuous affect dimensions.', ['Those are speech-data targets, not the listed face annotations.','Those are robot-control labels, not these annotations.','The slide explicitly lists labels and dimensions.'])
add(1582,'discrete-example','Reading the supplied seven-category example','Which pair belongs to the displayed discrete-category list rather than its continuous dimensions?', ['Disgust and surprise','Valence and arousal','Pitch and speaking rate','Confidence and angular radius'], 'Disgust and surprise are two named categories in the illustrated label set.', ['These are the continuous affect dimensions.','These are acoustic features.','These are model fields or internal representation quantities.'],kind='figure',assumptions=['Read the slide’s supplied labels; do not infer an unseen person’s internal emotion from a photograph.'])
add(1582,'ekman-qualification','Neutral label versus Ekman attribution','How should the slide’s seven-label list that includes neutral be described accurately?', ['As this classifier example’s label set, not an exact statement of Ekman’s seven universal-emotion list','As proof that neutral and contempt are interchangeable labels','As a list that contains no non-emotion baseline class','As evidence that every dataset must use precisely these seven labels'], 'The example includes neutral; Ekman’s seven-emotion list includes contempt instead, so the task label set and historical theory must be distinguished.', ['Neutral and contempt are not interchangeable categories.','Neutral is a baseline label in this example.','Different datasets define different label schemas.'],conflict='The slide attributes a seven-item list including neutral to Ekman; that conflates a common classification label set with the theory’s list.',primary=('Paul Ekman Group: Universal Emotions','https://www.paulekman.com/universal-emotions/','The six basic emotions and the seventh addition, contempt.'))
add(1583,'valence-arousal','Valence versus arousal','Which distinction is appropriate for the continuous affect dimensions?', ['Valence describes negative-to-positive quality; arousal describes calm-to-activated intensity','Both dimensions measure only image brightness','Valence describes activation and arousal only positive-versus-negative quality','Arousal is always positive and valence always negative'], 'Valence and arousal describe different aspects of affect rather than two copies of physical image energy.', ['Pixel brightness is not their affective meaning.','This swaps the two dimensions.','Both can take values across the stated negative-to-positive numeric scale.'],conflict='The slide says both measure energy levels and calls arousal calmness; valence is not energy, and arousal spans calm to activated.',primary=('AffectNet paper','https://arxiv.org/html/1708.03985v1','Introduction, paragraph defining valence and arousal.'))
add(1583,'label-numbers','Interpreting supplied continuous annotations','Using the supplied annotation valence0.63 and arousal0.77, which description matches the coordinate signs?', ['Positive valence and relatively high activation on the stated scale','Negative valence and low activation','Positive valence and a77% probability of truth','A63% certainty that the person has a specific diagnosis'], 'The two values are coordinates on the affect scale, not probabilities or diagnoses.', ['Both supplied values are positive, not negative.','Arousal is not a calibrated probability.','Valence is not diagnostic certainty.'],kind='figure',assumptions=['Interpret only the supplied numerical annotation; do not claim direct knowledge of the photographed person’s internal state.'])
add(1584,'axis-conflict','Resolving the scatter plot’s swapped axis labels','The left/right examples vary V from negative to positive and the bottom/top examples vary A; which mapping follows these numeric annotations?', ['Horizontal: valence; vertical: arousal','Horizontal: arousal; vertical: valence','Both axes: valence','Both axes: classifier confidence'], 'The printed V and A values vary across horizontal and vertical position respectively, contradicting the swapped outer labels.', ['This follows the inconsistent outer labels rather than the numeric examples.','The second coordinate varies independently as A.','The annotations are affect coordinates, not confidence.'],typ='diagram',kind='figure',conflict='The outer axis labels on the slide are swapped relative to the plotted V/A annotations; the question explicitly follows the numeric annotations.',primary=('AffectNet paper','https://arxiv.org/html/1708.03985v1','Figure1: sample images in the valence-arousal circumplex.'))
add(1584,'coordinate-quadrant','Locating a supplied V/A point','On axes x=valence and y=arousal, where is the annotated point V=−0.6,A=0.75?', ['Upper-left quadrant','Upper-right quadrant','Lower-left quadrant','Lower-right quadrant'], 'Negative x places the point left of the origin, and positive y places it above.', ['Valence is negative, so it is not on the right.','Arousal is positive, so it is not below.','Both signs conflict with lower-right.'],typ='diagram',kind='figure',assumptions=['Use the explicit x=V,y=A convention rather than the slide’s swapped outer labels.'])
add(1585,'ser-vs-asr','SER versus speech transcription','What distinguishes speech emotion recognition from automatic speech recognition?', ['SER estimates expressed affect from vocal patterns; ASR estimates the words spoken','SER translates text into audio; ASR directly measures internal feelings','SER and ASR always produce the same output labels','SER requires facial pixels as its only input'], 'The tasks can share audio input while targeting different outputs: affective cues versus lexical content.', ['Text-to-speech is synthesis; ASR is word recognition.','Word sequences and emotion estimates are different targets.','SER uses speech audio, although multimodal systems can add images.'])
add(1585,'ser-features','Acoustic cues used by SER','Which feature set matches the speech cues named on the slide?', ['Pitch, speaking rate, energy and MFCCs','Only punctuation and paragraph length','Only facial landmark distances','Only valence labels with no observed speech features'], 'These acoustic features capture aspects of how speech is produced.', ['Those are text features, not the listed acoustic cues.','Those are visual features.','Valence labels can be targets, but are not the listed observed acoustic inputs.'])
add(1585,'ser-pipeline','Speech-emotion pipeline','Which order follows the speech-emotion diagram?', ['Speech waveform → acoustic feature representation → learned model → estimated emotion','Estimated emotion → microphone → target waveform → ground-truth internal feeling','Text response → TTS → model training labels with no observation','Face crop → joint torque → word transcript → acoustic energy'], 'The model operates on features obtained from observed speech before producing an estimate.', ['The estimate is an output, not the initial observed speech.','This mixes speech synthesis with recognition.','These are not the linked stages in the shown audio pipeline.'],typ='diagram',kind='figure')
add(1585,'confidence-calibration','A displayed model percentage','What can a displayed HAPPY90% value establish without calibration evidence?', ['It is a model-reported score, not automatically a90% validated probability of the person’s true feeling','It proves the person’s internal happiness with90% certainty','It proves the classifier is correct on90% of every possible population','It means90% of the input waveform contains the word happy'], 'A displayed score needs validation and interpretation before it can support a calibrated probabilistic claim.', ['Internal emotion is not directly measured by the display.','One score does not establish population-wide accuracy.','The percentage is not a count of waveform content.'],kind='figure',conflict='The illustrative90% output is not accompanied by calibration or accuracy evidence.')
add(1586,'fusion','Joint multimodal representation','Which design most directly matches a joint multimodal representation?', ['Combine aligned face, voice and language features so the model can use their relationships','Use three independent outputs but discard two before every prediction','Replace missing voice data with the image width and treat it as speech','Require every channel to have identical raw units before learning'], 'Joint representations let information from different observed channels influence the estimate together.', ['Discarding two channels does not exploit their complementary relationships.','Image width is not a substitute for missing acoustic evidence.','Learned representations can combine channels with different native units.'])
add(1586,'missing-modality','Handling an unavailable modality','A camera is obscured while speech remains available; which claim about a multimodal system is justified?', ['It may use remaining cues if trained and designed for missing inputs, with uncertainty preserved','Fusion guarantees unchanged accuracy even without missing-input support','The absent image should be treated as confident neutral expression','Audio becomes a direct measurement of private feelings when video is missing'], 'Complementary inputs can help, but missing-modality robustness is a system property that must be tested.', ['The architecture label alone does not guarantee robustness.','Missing evidence is not a confident neutral observation.','Audio still supplies indirect cues rather than direct internal-state access.'],typ='scenario',conflict='Multimodal fusion can improve robustness, but improvement and missing-channel tolerance are not automatic guarantees.')
add(1587,'engine-role','Emotion engine inputs and outputs','Which role best matches the engine block in the diagram?', ['Integrate emotion-related observations into an estimate that informs behavior and dialogue','Replace every sensor with a fixed emotional label','Measure consciousness directly from the language-model hidden width','Convert all emotion labels directly into identical motor trajectories'], 'The engine links one or more recognition modules to response selection.', ['The block integrates observations rather than eliminating them.','Hidden width is not a consciousness measurement.','Behavior depends on context and policy, not one identical trajectory.'],typ='diagram',kind='figure')
add(1588,'memory-planning','Memory and dialogue planning','How are conversation memory and the current emotion estimate used together?', ['They supply prior context and current cues for planning an appropriate response','Memory replaces the need to inspect the current user input','The latest label proves every past estimate was correct','Memory is only a fixed list of motor calibration constants'], 'Past interaction context and current estimates jointly inform response planning.', ['The current interaction can change what response is appropriate.','A new estimate does not validate all earlier estimates.','The slide describes conversational information and previous interactions.'])
add(1588,'memory-scope','Memory is not exclusive to social robots','Which interpretation of the slide’s social-robot distinction is defensible?', ['Conversation memory and affect-aware planning are emphasized here, but memory is not exclusive to social robots','A general-purpose robot is incapable of retaining any information','Any device that stores one value is necessarily a social robot','Memory alone proves that a robot understands human feelings'], 'The slide highlights a role in social interaction rather than a universal exclusion of memory from other robots.', ['Many non-social systems retain state and history.','A single stored value does not define social capability.','Storage does not establish affective understanding.'],conflict='The opening distinction can be read too absolutely; conversation-oriented use is the emphasis, not exclusive ownership of memory.')
add(1589,'expression-output','Recognizing versus expressing affect','Which operation is an expression output rather than an emotion-recognition input?', ['Selecting coordinated speech style, eye LEDs and head motion for the response','Extracting pitch from incoming user audio','Classifying an observed face crop','Estimating affect from the user’s transcript'], 'These outputs generate the robot’s communicative behavior after response planning.', ['Incoming pitch extraction belongs to perception.','Face classification is recognition.','Transcript analysis is an input-side estimate.'])
add(1589,'consistent-expression','Coordinating expressive channels','Why should speech style, facial display and gesture be coordinated with the planned response?', ['Conflicting output cues can undermine an otherwise appropriate verbal response','Matching channels proves the robot genuinely feels the portrayed emotion','A gesture can replace all semantic content in every task','Every response must use the same maximum-intensity expression'], 'Expressive channels should support rather than contradict the intended communicative style.', ['Consistent simulation is not evidence of subjective experience.','Tasks differ in their need for words and gestures.','Expression strength should depend on context.'],typ='scenario')
add(1590,'recognition-challenges','Variation and mixed affect','Which issue directly challenges a single-label facial classifier in real interactions?', ['Different people and cultures express affect differently, and mixed states may not fit one label','A label vocabulary makes illumination and occlusion irrelevant','Every person expresses every category in exactly the same way','Mixed emotions are impossible once a softmax is used'], 'Variation, partial observations and mixtures limit how well a fixed categorical model represents real interactions.', ['Lighting and occlusion change the available visual evidence.','Uniform expression is not warranted.','Softmax is a model choice, not a rule about human experience.'])
add(1590,'privacy','Privacy in emotion-aware interaction','Which design response directly addresses the lecture’s privacy and ethical concerns?', ['Limit collected/stored cues to the intended task and communicate uncertainty and retention choices','Store every raw face and voice recording indefinitely because estimates may improve','Treat an inferred emotion as permission for unrelated profiling','Hide emotion-estimation behavior because the model uses a public architecture'], 'Data minimization, clear purpose and transparent uncertainty reduce avoidable privacy and misuse risks.', ['Indefinite collection is not justified merely by possible improvement.','An inferred state is not consent for a new purpose.','Architecture availability does not remove privacy responsibilities.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1590,'application-scope','Assistive application limits','For a healthcare or educational companion, which use stays within the lecture’s bounded emotion-aware role?', ['Adapt communication cautiously while leaving consequential judgments to appropriate human oversight','Diagnose a disorder from one facial category without further assessment','Rank a learner’s ability solely from an arousal estimate','Treat a low-confidence label as authorization to disclose private recordings'], 'The examples motivate supportive interaction, not unsupported diagnoses, eligibility judgments or disclosure.', ['A facial-expression category is not a diagnosis.','Arousal is not a valid sole measure of ability.','A model output supplies no disclosure permission.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
